{"name":"Jester Red","hex":"#ac112c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jester","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who jests, jokes or teases.","A person in colourful garb and fool's cap who amused a medieval and early modern royal or noble court."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To act in a foolish or clownish way for the purpose of entertainment or attracting attention."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jester"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jester","blocks":[{"text":"A jester, also known as joker, court jester, or fool, was a member of the household of a nobleman or a monarch kept to entertain guests at the royal court. Jesters were also travelling performers who entertained common folk at fairs and town markets, and the discipline continues into the modern day, where jesters perform at historical-themed events. Jester-like figures were common throughout the world, including ancient Rome, China, Persia, the Aztec Empire and Medieval Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Heinrich_Vogtherr_d._J._Schalksnarr.JPG/330px-Heinrich_Vogtherr_d._J._Schalksnarr.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jester","wikidata":"Q215548"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg/500px-Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg/500px-Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1404–1425, France. Two men smile at a jester's act, appearing to use a dog for a bagpipe.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_(Royal_15_D11_folio_262).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Schuttersfeest%2C_Meester_van_Frankfurt%2C_%281493%29%2C_Koninklijk_Museum_voor_Schone_Kunsten_Antwerpen%2C_529.jpg/500px-Schuttersfeest%2C_Meester_van_Frankfurt%2C_%281493%29%2C_Koninklijk_Museum_voor_Sch